```ADD_PAPER
2507.10734v2
In "Will anyone review this paper? Screening, sorting, and the feedback cycles that imperil peer review," the authors analyze the challenges in current peer review systems by modeling the feedback loops that arise as increasing submissions strain the capacity of qualified reviewers. Their work exposes how overburdened reviewers and ensuing declines in review accuracy can create a self-reinforcing cycle that undermines the overall quality of peer evaluation. Importantly, the paper discusses the potential for interventions—such as structured, bi-directional feedback mechanisms and incentive schemes—to disrupt these cycles. Experimental results reveal that implementing reviewer incentives and managing review workloads effectively leads to marked improvements in review quality. These insights are directly relevant to our goal of designing reasoning and prompt engineering techniques for accurately measuring peer-review quality signals and evaluating a two-stage, author-feedback review and reviewer reward system in AI conferences.
```ADD_PAPER
``` 
