2311.11967v1
In "Automatic Analysis of Substantiation in Scientific Peer Reviews," the authors develop an automated framework that employs argument mining to gauge how well claims in peer reviews are supported by evidence. The paper introduces a claim-evidence pair extraction method and constructs the SubstanReview dataset to benchmark substantiation levels across reviews. Notably, the experimental results demonstrate that their approach can accurately quantify variations in review quality, revealing systematic patterns in how reviewers structure their arguments. These findings suggest that integrating reasoning-guided methods—similar in spirit to advanced prompt engineering—can significantly refine the measurement of quality signals in peer review. This work provides valuable insights for designing a two-stage, bi-directional review process where initial reviewer comments are enhanced via targeted, automated analysis, ultimately underpinning robust reviewer reward systems in AI conferences.
```ADD_PAPER
``` 
