In "Paper Quality Assessment based on Individual Wisdom Metrics from Open Peer Review," the authors develop an open peer review framework that harnesses aggregated reviewer ratings and individual reputation metrics to produce a more reliable evaluation of manuscript quality. Their approach employs a Bayesian aggregation model that refines paper quality estimates based on each reviewer's historical performance, and it introduces a bidirectional feedback loop where authors assess the reviews they receive. Extensive experiments on datasets from major conferences demonstrate that this dual-layer assessment—combining quantitative reviewer signals with qualitative feedback—substantially improves the accuracy and fairness of paper evaluations. These findings are highly pertinent to our research goal of designing reasoning and prompt engineering techniques to maximize the accuracy of measuring peer-review quality signals and to support a robust two-stage bi-directional author-feedback review and reviewer reward system in AI conferences.
