In "DecentPeeR: A Self-Incentivised & Inclusive Decentralized Peer Review System," the authors propose a decentralized framework that rewards high-quality peer review behavior across venues using a comprehensive reputation model and game-theoretic analysis. Their approach establishes a Nash equilibrium where both authors and reviewers gain benefits from contributing constructively, effectively mitigating traditional biases in peer review. Extensive simulation experiments demonstrate significant improvements in the consistency and reliability of quality signals extracted from review data, as the system dynamically calibrates incentives based on reviewer performance. These findings provide a compelling complement to reasoning and prompt engineering techniques by highlighting how decentralized, incentive-compatible designs can bolster a two-stage, bidirectional author-feedback review process in AI conferences.
