Postdoc:  ```PLAN
Our experiment will investigate the benefits of a dual-model, two-stage peer review process for AI conferences by cross-validating quality signals in reviews. The plan is structured as follows:

1. Data Curation:
   • Use the arXiv API to collect a focused subset of computer science preprints that include mathematical formulas (to test math-aware typo detection) and cover diverse topics.
   • Preprocess papers to extract textual content, figures, and equations.
   
2. Model Setup:
   a. Review Generation Model:
      • Utilize GPT-4 (or a similar state-of-the-art LLM) to generate structured initial reviews. 
      • Design strict JSON-only prompts requiring two sections:
         – Section 1: A summary with strengths, key questions, and claim-evidence pairs (for substantiation analysis).
         – Section 2: Detection of typos (including math-aware filtering) and weaknesses with a final numeric rating.
   b. Meta-Review Quality Model:
      • Employ a second independently fine-tuned LLM, adapted (e.g., a GPT-4 variant fine-tuned on peer review corpora) for meta-analysis.
      • This model will parse the JSON output from the initial review, checking for internal consistencies, completeness of claim–evidence reporting, and overall quality.
      • Output a JSON report with quantitative metrics:
         – Typo error rate (errors per 1,000 words)
         – Substantiation score (accuracy and completeness of claim–evidence pairs)
         – Consistency indicators (noting mismatches in mathematical expressions or keywords)

3. Stage-2: Author Feedback Simulation:
   • Develop a simulation script that uses a rule-based yet stochastic rubric. This script will generate simulated author feedback by slightly modifying the original review based on:
         – Strengths or suggestions for improvement (with preset variation to mimic real human responses).
         – A simple scoring rubric for rebuttal sentiment.
   • Feed these simulated author responses back into the meta-review quality model which will re-calculate the quality metrics, yielding an updated review integrity score and revised badge eligibility (e.g., ranking top 10%/30% reviewers).

4. Experimental Design & Metrics:
   • Execute two pipelines on the same dataset: 
         A single-stage process (initial review only) and a two-stage process (with simulated author feedback).
   • Metrics to compare include:
         – Typo error rate improvements (pre and post author feedback)
         – Consistency scores in argument substantiation and math expression checks
         – Keyword churn dynamics (tracking top-20 keywords over paper subsets)
         – Reviewer reward simulation: calculating a reviewer impact score based on quality improvements after feedback and verifying badge thresholds’ stability.
   • Pre-register core hypotheses:
         H1: Typos and math-related errors reduce significantly after incorporating author feedback.
         H2: The two-stage process yields higher consistency and substantiation scores than the one-stage process.
         H3: The reviewer reward mechanism (badge thresholds) remains stable with authentic author-feedback simulation.
         H4: The dual-model setup more effectively deters low-effort automated reviews than a single-model pipeline.

5. Implementation & Output Enforcement:
   • Establish and test strict JSON schemas for all output types (initial reviews, meta-reviews, and author feedback) to ensure machine parsability and minimal free-form text.
   • Build an end-to-end pipeline that logs all outputs, tracks changes, and automates metric calculations via lightweight statistical analysis scripts (e.g., using bootstrapping for confidence intervals).
   • Run initial experiments at scale on a development cluster to ensure rapid iteration and later complement with a small, controlled sandbox trial (possibly inviting volunteer reviewers to simulate real author feedback).

This dual-model, two-stage pilot combines rigorous prompt engineering with innovative meta-review analysis to quantify and improve peer-review quality signals, paving the way for a robust bi-directional reviewer reward system across AI conferences.
  
By rapidly shipping this end-to-end pipeline, we can iterate quickly on model choices, prompt refinements, and metric calculations.
  
End of plan.
```PLAN 
~~~~~~~~~~~
